    "## 3. Match the paper's `Type` encoding\n",
    "The paper encodes `Type` as **H = 0, L = 1, M = 2**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Our `preprocess.py` uses **L = 0, M = 1, H = 2**.\n",
    "\n",
    "To reproduce the paper, we change the numbers here to the paper's numbers. This is a fixed rule (not learned from data), so it causes **no data leakage**. We apply the same rule to train and test.\n",
    "\n",
    "> If the team later changes `encode_type()` in `preprocess.py` to the paper's mapping, set `USE_PAPER_TYPE_ENCODING = False`. Otherwise the mapping would be applied twice."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "d71ed396",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-11T13:17:18.371265Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-11T13:17:18.371125Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-11-11T13:17:18.377734Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-11-11T13:17:18.376685Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "type\n",
      "0     705\n",
      "1    4217\n",
      "2    2078\n",
      "Name: count, dtype: int64\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# [Purpose]: Match the paper's Type encoding (L=0, M=1, H=2 -> H=0, L=1, M=2).\n",
    "# Remap the current values: 0 (L) -> 1, 1 (M) -> 2, and 2 (H) -> 0.\n",
    "USE_PAPER_TYPE_ENCODING = True\n",
    "\n",
    "if USE_PAPER_TYPE_ENCODING:\n",
    "    type_map = {0: 1, 1: 2, 2: 0}\n",
    "    X_train[\"type\"] = X_train[\"type\"].map(type_map)\n",
    "    X_test[\"type\"] = X_test[\"type\"].map(type_map)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(X_train[\"type\"].value_counts().sort_index())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cb41ec4f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Scaling and data leakage\n",
    "The paper applies **Min-Max Normalization** to the 5 sensor features and leaves `Type` unscaled.\n",
    "\n",
    "`build_pipeline(model, mode=\"paper\")` does exactly this. It puts `MinMaxScaler` and the model into one scikit-learn `Pipeline`:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `pipeline.fit(X_train, y_train)` -> the scaler learns min/max from **training data only**\n",
    "- `pipeline.predict(X_test)` -> the test data is only **transformed** with the training min/max\n",
    "\n",
    "So the test data never influences the scaler. The saved CSV files are unscaled, which is why we scale here."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b64a113a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Decision Tree\n",
    "Paper Table II: `min_samples_split = 2`\n",
    "\n",
    "We add `random_state=42` only so that we get the same tree every time we run the notebook.\n",
    "\n",
    "A Decision Tree does not need scaling, but we use the same pipeline for all models to keep the same preprocessing as the paper."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "3f791a7f",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-11-11T13:17:18.379246Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-11-11T13:17:18.379106Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-11-11T13:17:18.434297Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-11-11T13:17:18.433930Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Decision Tree trained.\n",
      "Tree depth: 19\n",
      "Predicted failures in test: 119 of 3000\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Purpose: Fit the Decision Tree model and predict labels for the test data.\n",
    "dt_model = build_pipeline(\n",
    "    DecisionTreeClassifier(min_samples_split=2, random_state=RANDOM_STATE),\n",
    "    mode=\"paper\",\n",
    ")\n",
    "dt_model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
    "y_pred_decision_tree = dt_model.predict(X_test)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Decision Tree trained.\")\n",
    "print(\"Tree depth:\", dt_model.named_steps[\"model\"].get_depth())\n",
    "print(\"Predicted failures in test:\", y_pred_decision_tree.sum(), \"of\", len(y_pred_decision_tree))"
   ]
  },
